        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Adwa</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">1 March 1896</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the town of Adwa, in the Tigray region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, on the mountains and in the valleys west and south of the town</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, with estimates of between 73,000 and over 100,000 men; an Italian colonial force under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri">Oreste Baratieri</a>, estimated at 14,519 effective combat troops by Harold Marcus and at 17,770 with fifty-six artillery pieces by David Levering Lewis</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">An Ethiopian victory: over 6,000 Italian and colonial troops were killed and around 3,800 captured, by one set of figures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> later signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a>, recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as independent.</span></div>
