    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The war began from a dispute over Article 17 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a>, which the Italian text read as making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> text read as an offer of Italy's good offices.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri">Baratieri</a> attacked under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and from his own generals, and his columns, advancing on poor maps, lost touch with one another and were defeated one after another.</li><li>Prisoners were treated unequally: about 800 captured Eritrean askari were mutilated, and around 70 Italian prisoners were massacred in retaliation for the death of Bashah Aboye.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Italian army at Adwa consisted mainly of white European troops, unlike most European armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>, and the newer Carcano Model 91 rifles were not issued according to Chris Prouty, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri">Baratieri</a> was under constraints to be economical and wanted to use up the old cartridges.</p></aside>
    </section>

